course: biology
lesson: 39
title: Octopuses, squid and cuttlefish
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Octopuses, squid and cuttlefish are molluscs, cousins of snails, whose shells have shrunk inside or vanished, which turned them into soft, clever, shape-changing hunters.
explain: An octopus has eight arms, three hearts and blue blood, and no bones at all except a parrot-like beak, so it can squeeze through a hole the size of a coin.
explain: About two thirds of its nerve cells are in its arms, so each arm can feel, taste and make simple decisions on its own while it explores a different rock.
explain: It can open a jar to reach a crab, turn sandy grey to vanish against a rock, and squirt a cloud of ink, the same black that colours squid ink pasta.
explain: Cartoon octopuses wave eight «tentacles» and kidnap ships, but scientists call octopus limbs arms, giant squid live far below in the dark, and even the colossal squid's eye, as big as a football, is real, not a monster story.
fact: An octopus has eight arms, three hearts and blue blood: two hearts pump blood through the gills and the third round the body.
fact: The octopus's body heart stops while it swims, so swimming tires it quickly, and octopuses mostly crawl over the rocks instead.
fact: An octopus has no bones; its only hard part is a parrot-like beak, so it can squeeze through any gap bigger than the beak.
fact: An octopus has about 500 million nerve cells, about two thirds of them in its arms, and its suckers can taste what they touch.
fact: Octopuses can solve puzzles, open jars, learn by watching and remember people; wild ones carry coconut shell halves to hide in.
fact: In 2016 an octopus called Inky escaped from an aquarium in New Zealand: he slipped through a small gap at the top of his tank, crossed the floor and slid down a drainpipe into the sea.
fact: Most octopuses seem to be colour-blind, yet they match the colours around them very well, and scientists are still working out how.
fact: Octopus skin has thousands of tiny colour bags it can open and close in a fraction of a second, and it can raise bumps to copy the texture of rock.
fact: Octopuses, squid and cuttlefish squirt ink made of melanin to hide or to make a fake blob while they jet away; cooks use squid ink to colour pasta black.
fact: Colossal squid have the largest eyes known of any animal, about 27 cm across, and the giant squid was first filmed alive in its deep home only in 2012.
fact: Sperm whales dive deep to hunt giant squid, and many sperm whales carry round scars left by the squid's suckers.
fact: A squid moves fast by jet propulsion: it squirts water out through a funnel called a siphon and shoots the other way.
fact: The cuttlebone is a cuttlefish's inside shell, full of tiny chambers of gas and liquid that help it float or sink; budgies peck it for calcium.
fact: The blue-ringed octopus is only golf-ball size, but its venom can kill an adult; its blue rings flash as a warning, so never pick up an octopus from a rock pool.
fact: A mother octopus guards and cleans her eggs without eating until they hatch, and soon after she dies; one deep-sea mother guarded hers for four and a half years.
fact: The nautilus is an octopus relative that still has an outside coiled shell with gas-filled chambers and around 90 tentacles without suckers.
exercise: How many hearts does an octopus have? | Three | One | Two | Eight
exercise: What colour is an octopus's blood? | Blue | Red | Green | Black
exercise: How can an octopus squeeze through a hole the size of a coin? | It has no bones; only its beak is hard | It folds all its bones up like an umbrella | It breaks its bones and then regrows them on the other side | It shrinks by breathing all its air out
exercise: Why does each arm of an octopus seem to explore a rock on its own? | Two thirds of its nerve cells are in its arms | Each arm has its own small eye | The arms are separate animals joined together | The sea current moves the arms
exercise: What can an octopus's suckers do besides grip? | Taste what they touch | See the colours around them | Breathe air out of the water | Hear sounds under the water
exercise: An octopus in an aquarium meets a jar with a crab inside. What can it do? | Unscrew the lid to reach the crab | Nothing, it cannot open anything with its soft arms | Smash the glass jar with its hard bones | Wait for the crab to climb out by itself
exercise: How does an octopus turn from red to sandy grey? | By opening and closing thousands of tiny colour bags in its skin | By swallowing sand | By holding its breath | By shedding its skin
exercise: Why does an octopus squirt ink? | To hide or make a fake blob while it escapes | To write messages to the other octopuses nearby | To clean the water | To catch fish in the black cloud
exercise: What makes squid ink pasta black? | Ink made of melanin from squid or cuttlefish | Burnt flour | Black olives | Charcoal powder from the bread oven
exercise: How big is the eye of a colossal squid? | About 27 cm across, the size of a football | About 2 cm, like a marble | About 2 metres across, as big as a door | As small as a pinhead
exercise: When was the giant squid first filmed alive in its deep home? | In 2012 | In 1912 | In 1969 | It has never been filmed
exercise: How does a squid shoot backwards like a rocket? | It squirts water out through its siphon | It flaps all eight of its arms up and down very fast | It pushes off from rocks | It burps out air
exercise: What is the white cuttlebone in a budgie's cage? | The inside shell of a cuttlefish | A dried bit of a big fish's backbone | A piece of coral | Dried squid ink
exercise: What does a cuttlefish use its cuttlebone for? | To float up or sink by changing the gas inside | To fight with other cuttlefish in the breeding season | To dig in the sand | To store food
exercise: Why does a blue-ringed octopus flash its blue rings? | As a warning to stay away | To attract a mate in the dark | To light up the dark rock pool | Because it is happy and calm
exercise: How dangerous is the bite of a blue-ringed octopus? | It can kill an adult person | It feels like a gentle tickle | It is only dangerous to crabs | It has no bite at all, only suckers
exercise: What does an octopus mother do after laying her eggs? | Guards and cleans them without eating until they hatch | Leaves them and swims away | Carries them about on her back for a year until they are big | Eats half of them
exercise: What is different about a nautilus? | It still has an outside coiled shell | It has no tentacles or arms of any kind at all | It lives on land | It has bones like a fish
test: In a cartoon, an octopus with two hearts and red blood waves eight tentacles. What would a marine biologist correct? | Three hearts, blue blood, and its limbs are called arms | Nothing, that is all correct | It should have one big heart and bright green blood | It should have ten legs and wings
test: A crab is hiding under a rock where the octopus cannot see it. How might the octopus still find it? | Its arm suckers taste the crab when they touch it | It hears the crab's heart beating | It smells it from a mile away | It cannot find hidden food
test: A keeper at an aquarium keeps getting squirted with water by one octopus, but other keepers don't. Why might that be? | Octopuses remember people and may squirt ones they dislike | The octopus is blind and simply squirts at anyone who comes near it | The keeper smells of crab | Octopuses squirt every keeper the same
test: Your mate says octopuses are colour-blind so they can't match colours. What's the truth? | Most seem colour-blind, yet they match colours very well, and scientists are still working out how | They see better colours than people | They are not colour-blind at all | They never change colour
test: What would happen if a squid's siphon were pointed forwards when it squirted? | It would shoot backwards, the opposite way to the water | It would shoot forwards too, in the same direction as the water | It would not move at all | It would sink straight down
test: If an octopus's main heart stops when it swims, why might it prefer to crawl? | Swimming tires it quickly, so crawling is easier | Crawling over the rocks is much faster than swimming in open water | Its arms cannot swim | Swimming makes it change colour
test: oi, can i grab that cute little octopus with blue rings in the rock pool? | No, its bite can kill, so never pick it up | Yes, it is harmless | Yes, but only if you hold it gently with wet hands | Only if it is sleeping
test: Inky escaped from a New Zealand aquarium. How did he manage it? | He slipped through a gap in his tank and down a drainpipe into the sea | A keeper let him go by mistake | He broke the thick glass with the hard bones in his arms | He was carried out in a bucket
test: A predator lunges at a dark blob in the water, but the squid has gone. What happened? | The squid squirted an ink blob and jetted away | The squid turned into ink | The blob was another squid | The squid hid inside the predator's open mouth
test: A cuttlefish lets more gas into its cuttlebone. Which way will it go? | Up | Down | Backwards | Nowhere
test: A sperm whale washes ashore with round scars on its skin. What is a likely cause? | Suckers of a giant squid it hunted | Barnacles from a ship | Bites from a crowd of small sharks | Sunburn
test: Why is the colossal squid's eye so huge? | To catch the faintest light in the dark deep sea | Because it lives in bright shallow water | To scare divers | So it can see the stars
exam: Identify the anatomical feature which permits an octopus to pass through apertures of very small dimension. | The absence of any skeleton save a beak | Collapsible bones within each arm | A shell which may be detached | The ability to dissolve its tissues and to reform them afterwards
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the distribution of nerve cells in the octopus? | The majority reside within the arms | All reside within the central brain | They are confined to the eyes | They are absent from the arms
exam: Ascertain the function of the cuttlebone within the living cuttlefish. | The regulation of buoyancy by gas and liquid | The production of ink | The digestion of shellfish | The defence against predators by stinging
exam: It is given that an octopus has deposited her eggs. Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning her subsequent conduct. | She guards them without feeding until hatching and dies soon after | She abandons them at once | She consumes them during winter | She transfers them to the father, who carries them until hatching
